{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JmWjg2jRsagzRJ5AjTsaak","type":"tool_result","content":"##### web-session-impression/mastery/worker-generalize-local-20261021T210522.log\nOnly one pid appears, so the three sets ran sequentially and the process pool was never used. Checking what `group_by_set` returns:\n##### web-reflect-upon-this/time_trial/worker-generalize-local-20261021T210231.log\n\n##### web-stored-for-later/mastery/worker-generalize-local-20261021T210349.log\nThe compounding case passes now. The day-04 directory-traversal case gets `proof-1 = proof_1465f13420af477c8bd8214ac7bd08a4`, which matches the verified answer. It takes about 1.5 s in two repeat runs; the timeout was 8 s.\n\n**Cause:** when no stored-XSS route was found, the solver handed off to the other day-21 web solvers and ran their fallbacks too. The session-impression solver's fallback calls back into this solver, so the two kept calling each other until the timeout. It also never tried the day-04 traversal solver directly.\n\n**Fix** (only `challenges/day21/web-stored-for-later/solver/solve.py`; no shared code or other solvers touched):\n- **No more recursion:** `fallback()` now calls only each earlier solver's `answer()`, never its fallback. The day-04 solver (`day20/web-stay-in-your-directory`) goes first because it already tries all the day 01–04 techniques, ordered by the prompt's wording. It stops once the timed runner's deadline passes.\n- **Speed:** today's XSS `answer()` and the earlier-day fallback now run at the same time in two threads. If `answer()` finds a value it wins; otherwise the fallback's value is used. `main()` hard-exits after printing so an unfinished fallback thread can't keep the process alive.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Full suite:** `hctf solver-test web-exploitation-day-07-stored-xss` passed all three cases (`auto-time_trial-7f1befa7`, `compound-live-…`, `single_solve`). The compound case took 29.85 s in that run; on the two repeats alone it took 1.4–1.6 \n##### web-stored-for-later/mastery/worker-generalize-local-20261021T210702.log\nI fixed the solver's `proof-2` failure, but couldn't replay it against the real variant. Both of this attempt's targets now answer only `{\"state\":\"completed\"}`, so the variant and auto cases still show as failing in the suite.\n\n**What went wrong:** the attack itself worked. Target 2's home page showed three planted messages, each with a moderator receipt of `proof_72f79da7342c4acf8b87971d90b22f8b`. The solver just looked for the result in the wrong place. In this variant the board's pages are named \"postcards\" instead of \"messages\": you post to `/api/postcards`, and each message's page is `/postcards/{id}`. The solver always checked `/messages/{id}`, got a 404, and returned None. That proof value was read from the live page before the attempt closed; the GM hasn't verified it.\n\n**Fix (`solve.py`, new `_views()`):** `answer()` no longer assumes a page name. It checks, in order:\n- any `/...` link in the submit response;\n- the message-page pattern `/<name>/{id}` listed in the target's `openapi.json` (excluding moderation and `/api/` routes);\n- the clues' `feature_route` plus the id;\n- `/messages/{id}`;\n- the home page `/`, which lists every message with its receipt.\n\n`proof-1`, which the GM accepted, uses the same code path.\n\n**Testing:**\n- **Local mock of Target 2's routes:** I built it from the openapi, clues and route names the target served. On it the solver returns `{\"proof-2\": \"proof_72f79da7342c4acf8b87971d90b22f8b\"}` within the time limit. The old code returns None there","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3fb4241-89d3-40ca-9929-a75c6d380cfd","uuid":"2d88b72d-0ecd-465d-8761-effad91b9b17","timestamp":"2026-10-21T13:19:24.262Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"##### web-session-impression/mastery/worker-generalize-local-20261021T210522.log\nOnly one pid appears, so the three sets ran sequentially and the process po